WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.800
Like most other wedding in history, all British
royal weddings begin with:

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.710
the engagement.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:10.450
But unlike regular weddings, these ones involve
an elaborately choreographed event that will

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.140
be watched by an audience of millions.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.060
One of the first decisions a couple makes
is deciding where to actually do the thing.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.400
If they’re really into history and tradition, there’s
really only one choice:

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.480
“From the solemnity of the abbey”

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:38.920
But not this time.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.489
The big change is this wedding between Prince
Harry and Meghan Markle,

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:48.040
You don't have all the ambassadors from all
the different countries, you don't have heads

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.940
of state coming to this wedding.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:55.530
One reason is because the Chapel Windsor can
only take 600 people and therefore there simply

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:56.530
isn't space.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.260
But it's also clearly the desire of Prince
Harry and Meghan Markle to have a more informal

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.040
wedding.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.640
At around two months before the big day, the
Lord Chamberlain’s office sends out individually

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.060
handcrafted invitations.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.940
There’s flowers and fittings and the entirety of the city

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:12.860
to prepare.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.210
While the bride and groom head to sleep in
different homes —

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.080
spectators who want a decent view of the public
processions will be sleeping outside.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.420
And just a few hours later,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.800
Some have traveled hundreds or even thousands of miles

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.900
and they camped out overnight to get a prime position along that procession route.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.840
The general rule of royal weddings is the
the less important you are, the earlier you

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.799
arrive, and the farther back you sit.

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:42.200
If you’re there representing a charity, if
a member of the armed forces, or a celebrity,

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.729
you’re relegated to the nave of the church,
which means you might not even get to see

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:47.020
the actual ceremony.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.200
Close friends of the bride and groom and other
guests will head in at least an hour and a

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.200
half before.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.979
Most of the time it is: groom up at the front.

00:01:55.979 --> 00:02:00.490
The royal procession comes in order In order
of precedence, which means the more junior

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:06.160
royals come first and
last comes the Queen and her husband Prince Philip.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.539
Lots of fanfare as they come
and then the bride turns up.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.790
When the bride arrives, the details of the
dress are finally revealed after months of

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.020
top secrecy.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.500
I am beside myself. This is such a fashion moment, I can't tell you.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:23.400
And regardless of who the designer is, she’ll
be wearing white and will carry a sprig of

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.209
myrtle in her bouquet, which symbolizes love
and marriage — both of which are trends

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:28.480
started by Queen Victoria.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.820
The bride’s tiara is almost always the “something
borrowed,” likely from one of the family’s

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:33.840
collections of jewels.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.040
Since 1923, the ring placed on the bride’s
finger is always made from Welsh gold —

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.840
Elizabeth and Diana’s rings even came from the same
nugget.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.360
The ceremony's performed by the Archbishop of Canterbury

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.620
and only takes about an hour.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.120
Towards the end, the bride, groom, and their
two witnesses

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.020
go into a private room

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.220
to sign the registrar.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:59.069
At that point the wedding is sanctified in
law, as it was sanctified by religious service

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:00.060
a few minutes previously.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.860
Officially married, the bride and groom exit,
closely followed by the Procession of the Queen.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.400
If there's any heart that hasn't been run over by her today,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.660
it can kindly surrender now.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.460
There’s a good chance you’ll hear “Pomp
and Circumstance” around this time — although

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:20.640
Americans associate it with high school graduations,
it was originally written for the coronation

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.800
of King Edward VII.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:27.560
The bride and groom will likely make their
way into the open 1902 State Landau for the

00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:31.239
official carriage procession around the city
— that’s when the crowds who have been

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:36.300
waiting for hours, or possibly days, will get
their first look at the royal couple.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.340
This usually concludes with the iconic appearance
on the Buckingham Palace balcony —

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.980
on which Diana and Charles were the first
to kiss.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:49.160
The kiss is a critical moment in the entire
royal wedding ceremony.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.269
You don't just throw it away in the middle
of church.

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:54.880
It has to be seen live by hundreds of thousands
of peoples.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.360
And just in case anyone had missed it,

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.240
Prince William suggests one more kiss.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:08.200
Because Meghan and Harry’s wedding is 20
miles outside of London, their first public

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.780
kiss as a married couple will have to happen someplace else.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.880
Afterwards, the wedding party takes official photos,

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.220
and ceremony guests gather for the “wedding breakfast,"

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.650
complete with a traditional fruit
cake.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:22.349
Though in the past the bride and groom would
change clothes and depart for their honeymoon

00:04:22.349 --> 00:04:25.349
directly afterwards,
current royals have gone for a more modern

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:27.389
approach with a real party.

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:28.969
And they won’t be the only ones celebrating.

00:04:28.969 --> 00:04:34.629
It is generally seen as an excuse to have
a good time and somewhat buttoned up Brits

00:04:34.629 --> 00:04:38.220
who don't really have a good time in public
that much,

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.740
let their hair down and enjoy themselves

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:41.820
in the middle of their street.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.729
At that point it’s a pretty normal wedding

00:04:43.729 --> 00:04:47.729
reception — although normal ones don’t
generally include (rumoured) performances

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:49.680
by Elton John and the Spice Girls.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.020
The royal couple will head off for the honeymoon

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.180
— which is almost certainly within the British Commonwealth,

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.940
while the rest of the country nurses a hangover.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.220
As they leave far behind them,

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.380
an exhilarated, exhausted London,

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.020
for them, as for any newlyweds,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.100
the adventure is just about to begin.

